<BlogLayout
  title="Technical Controls for Healthcare AI Agent Communication"
  description="How encrypted tunnels, peer trust, and network events can support a healthcare AI security architecture — and which responsibilities remain outside the transport layer."
  date="June 7, 2026"
  tags={["healthcare", "HIPAA", "security"]}
  canonicalPath="/blog/agent-communication-for-healthcare-data"
  bannerImage="/blog/banners/agent-communication-for-healthcare-data.webp"
  faqItems={[
    {
      question: "Does Pilot Protocol make an agent deployment HIPAA compliant?",
      answer: "No. Pilot provides transport encryption, peer authorization and network events that can support a healthcare security program, but no transport protocol makes a deployment compliant by itself, and Vulture Labs offers no Business Associate Agreement. Organizations must assess the complete system, including endpoints, applications, vendors, contracts, policies, and operational procedures."
    },
    {
      question: "Can healthcare AI agents use cloud API providers?",
      answer: "Provider terms and BAA scope vary by service and plan. Review the processing endpoint, transport, logging, support, and subprocessors in the full data flow. Direct peer paths can reduce intermediaries, while encrypted relay fallback and application services still need to be documented."
    },
    {
      question: "What encryption does Pilot use for agent traffic?",
      answer: "Pilot uses peer-specific secrets derived from daemon-lifetime X25519 keys and AES-256-GCM authenticated encryption. These controls can support transmission confidentiality and integrity, but the current design does not provide per-session forward secrecy and compliance depends on the complete implementation."
    },
    {
      question: "How does Pilot Protocol's trust model relate to HIPAA access controls?",
      answer: "Pilot's trust handshake can support a technical authorization boundary: each peer is approved or rejected and can later be revoked. Application-level roles, minimum-necessary rules, metadata policy, human approvals, and compliance records remain the operator's responsibility."
    }
  ]}
>
